      "text": "To solve this problem, we'll need to consider the following information:\n\n- The train's front enters the tunnel at time $t=0$.\n- The train travels at a speed of 60 miles per hour.\n- The tail exits the tunnel 3 minutes (or 180 seconds) after the front entered the tunnel.\n- The length of the train is 1 mile.\n\nWe can analyze the situation in two parts: the time it takes for the train to travel from the point where the front entered the tunnel until it reaches the tunnel's exit, and the time it takes for the tail of the train to exit the tunnel when traveling at its maximum speed.\n\nLet's calculate the time it takes for the train to travel from the point where the front entered until it reaches the tunnel's exit. Since the train travels at a speed of 60 miles per hour, we can convert this to miles per minute: $60$ miles per hour $\\times \\frac{1}{60}$ hours per minute $= 1$ mile per minute. Thus, it takes: $3\\text{ minutes} \\times 1 \\text{ mile per minute} = 3 \\text{ miles}$ for the train to exit the tunnel from the point where the front entered.\n\nNow, let's analyze the tail-exit problem. As the tail exits, the 1-mile long tail has been traveling an extra 2 miles. Hence, the length of the tunnel must therefore be $1 \\text{ mile} + 2 \\text{ miles} = 3 \\text{ miles}$.\n\nSo, the tunnel length must be 3 miles. We'll write the result below.\n\n\\(\\boxed{3}\\) miles\nLet's put this reasoning into a Python code to confirm our result.\n```python\n# Calculate the length of the tunnel\r\n# The train travels at a speed of 60 miles per hour\r\nspeed_60_mph = 60  # in miles per hour\r\n# Time in minutes when the train travels to the tunnel's exit\r\ntime_to_exit_minutes = 180  # 3 minutes\r\n\r\n# Convert speed to miles per minute\r\nspeed_mph = speed_60_mph / 60  # in miles per minute\r\n\r\n# Distance the train travels to exit the tunnel\r\ndistance_to_exit = time_to_exit_minutes * speed_mph\r\n\r\n# Tail-exit calculation\r\ntail_exit_distance = speed_mph * time_to_exit_minutes\r\n\r\n# Length of the tunnel\r\ntunnel_length = distance_to_exit + (tail_exit_distance - distance_to_exit)\r\n\r\nprint(tunnel_length)\n```",
