      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. Convert the train's speed from miles per hour (mph) to miles per minute (mpm).\n2. Determine how far the train travels in the 3 minutes it takes to exit the tunnel.\n3. Subtract this distance from 1 mile to find the length of the tunnel.\n\nStep 1: Convert the train's speed\n- The train moves at 60 miles per hour (mph).\n- There are 60 minutes in an hour, so to convert mph to mpm, divide by 60.\n\\[ \\text{Speed in mpm} = \\frac{60 \\text{ miles/hour}}{60 \\text{ minutes/hour}} = 1 \\text{ mile/minute} \\]\n\nStep 2: Calculate the distance traveled by the train\n- In 3 minutes, the train covers this distance: \\( \\text{Distance} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time} \\).\n- So, for 3 minutes: \n\\[ \\text{Distance} = 1 \\text{ mile/minute} \\times 3 \\text{ minutes} = 3 \\text{ miles} \\]\n\nStep 3: Determine the length of the tunnel\n- Since the tail of the train exits the tunnel exactly 3 minutes after the front of the train enters, the distance traveled by the train (3 miles) exits through the tunnel.\n- Given that the train is 1 mile long, the remaining distance must be the length of the tunnel.\n\\[ \\text{Length of the tunnel} = 1 \\text{ mile} - 3 \\text{ miles} = -2 \\text{ miles} \\]\n\nHowever, this result is incorrect due to misunderstanding the question's context. The correct interpretation is that the tail exits the tunnel 3 miles from the tunnel's entrance, but the train is 1 mile long. Therefore, the tunnel's length would be the distance traveled minus the train's length.\n\\[ \\text{Length of the tunnel} = 3 \\text{ miles} - 1 \\text{ mile} = 2 \\text{ miles} \\]\n\nFinal Answer: \\(\\boxed{2}\\) miles.",
